  "title": "Rival colonies, on by default in the bed you play",
  "question": "This is what a new box does now. Is that the bed you want to open - and when a colony loses, can you see it happen, or does it just quietly have fewer ants at the end?",
  "context_md": "**You said ship it on, so it is on.** `ant.ron` authors `scent_spread: 2.0`. Two clicks found two families instead of one, and strangers are *food* to each other's ordinary mouth before they are ever a target for the fight verb - which is why this reads as predation rather than as a battle.\n\n**Both strips: same bed, same seed, same 4,200 frames, same ground.** A is the bed as it was; B is what a new box gives you today. Yellow is ANT 1, blue ANT 2; they meet around the middle plant.\n\n- **A** - off. **0 killings between colonies.**\n- **B** - on, by default. **8 killings between colonies.**\n\n**Why 2.0.** The dial only decides how many world seeds start the two colonies far enough apart to recognise each other as strangers at all - past that line it changes nothing, because \"is this my family\" is a yes/no, not a dial. Over 12 seeds: **off 0 of 12** beds see a killing, **1.0 gets 9 of 12**, **2.0 gets 11 of 12**. One bed in twelve never separates at any setting and I could not fix that without a design change.\n\n**What it costs, same seeds, paired**: ~99 more deaths a run (up on 11 of 12 seeds), and starvation drops 4.3 points as a share - the killing *displaces* starving rather than adding to it. Population and births do not move at all.\n\n**Still yours to rule on.** Only COMMON ANT is switched on - the held world's other five foundable ants are still one family with themselves. And jarring an ant and releasing it beside its own nest now makes it a stranger its nestmates will eat, which follows from how release already worked and was invisible while the dial was 0. Set it to 0 anywhere to get the old bed back exactly.",
